  "recommendation": {
    "verdict": "DECLINE AS STRUCTURED - ONLY THE AGGRESSIVE, UNPROVEN DEMAND CASE CLEARS THE WACC",
    "stance": "decline_with_boundary",
    "detail": "The realistic revenue reform (R2: six external covers/day, +5% ADR, one FTE) produces EUR 71,321 of mature EBITDA and EUR 839,075 of asset value. Against the EUR 1,050,100 CapEx that is a gap of EUR -211,025; against total project investment of EUR 1,138,625 the gap is EUR -299,550. After 3.0% exit costs the net gap is EUR -324,723. R2 still has a project NPV of EUR -220,685 and a project IRR of 2.53%.",
    "detail_2": "The 900k value-engineered case (R3) improves the NPV to EUR -96,378 and the IRR to 5.37%, but still does not clear an 8% WACC. The 850k lean case (R5) reaches EUR -49,106 of NPV and 6.61% IRR, still below the hurdle. The only positive-NPV case is R4 (eight covers/day, +10% ADR, one FTE) at EUR 3,722 of NPV and 8.08% IRR - a negligible margin and a total-investment value gap of EUR -51,534. After 3.0% exit costs the net gap is EUR -84,147, so even the boundary case fails the net test. The combined downside (R6) is deeply negative: EBITDA EUR -33,887, NPV EUR -1,021,973. The value frontier does show a positive corner at roughly EUR 700k of CapEx (land included) with six or more covers, but that is EUR 450k of non-land spend for an eight-room refurbishment: a scope-reduction thesis, not the current 1.05M budget.",
    "conditions": [
      "Do not treat the 1.05M eight-room project as the base case for a reform.",
      "Prove external F&B demand before committing construction capital: a low-cost Phase 0 pop-up/restaurant test, not a full refurbishment.",
      "If the family wants to proceed, cap the initial capital at the lean scope and keep the second phase behind a demand gate.",
      "Treat the ~700k frontier corner as a different, smaller project: it only works by cutting the non-land budget to about 450k, which is not the eight-room refurbishment currently specified.",
      "Charge the land at its opportunity value; do not describe the project as viable only because the site is already owned.",
      "Price the exit net of selling costs. The gross cap-rate value flatters every case; the boundary case fails once 3% exit costs are deducted.",
      "Re-run the model with 1.25-1.5 FTE for the external covers, not one FTE, and with INE 21.24% occupancy in the downside."
    ],
